# Miniforge.ai Header & Copyright

<version>2.0.0</version>

## Scope — OSS repositories only

**This rule applies exclusively to Apache-2.0 licensed (open source) repositories.**

Detection: the repository root must contain a `LICENSE` file whose content includes the string `"Apache"`. If no such file exists, or the file contains `"Proprietary"` / `"All Rights Reserved"`, **do not apply this rule**.

> **Why `remediation.strategy: manual` and `autoFixable: false`:** two independent reasons, both blocking.
>
> 1. The Apache-license gate above is a human-judgement check that cannot be encoded in the frontmatter's `detection.*` fields. An automated runner must not prepend a copyright block without confirming the target is an OSS repo.
> 2. **The frontmatter format cannot express this header as a template.** Quoted scalars are read literally — no escape processing, and no block-scalar (`|`) syntax — so a multi-line value is inexpressible. A `\n` written in `remediation.template` stays a literal backslash and `n`, and a `mechanical` prepend would write the whole header as one line of literal `\n` characters. Because that line still contains both `Christopher Lester` and the email address, `detection` would then report the corrupted file **compliant** — silent corruption that satisfies its own gate.
>
> `remediation.template` is therefore **not set**: [`meta/rule-format`](../meta/rule-format.mdc) defines it as the text to insert when `remediation.type` is `prepend`/`append`, and this rule declares no `type`. The canonical block lives in [Requirements](#requirements) below, where real newlines work and it can be copied. Do not reintroduce `template`, `type: prepend`, `strategy: mechanical` or `autoFixable: true` until the format can represent a multi-line literal **and** the license-scope check above is machine-checkable.
>
> **Why `*.md` is not in the globs:** Root-level markdown files such as `CLAUDE.md`, `agents.md`, `CONTRIBUTING.md`, `CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md`, and `SECURITY.md` are structural files, not published content. The `docs/` and `specs/` globs cover the content directories where published articles live.

Proprietary Thesium products use `standards/thesium/project/header-proprietary` instead. Never apply an Apache 2.0 copyright header to a proprietary file.

## Context
- Use when authoring or editing site, docs, blog, or README content in an Apache-licensed repo.

## Requirements

- Copy this block verbatim to the very top of the file, before the first heading:

```html
<!--
  Title: Miniforge.ai
  Author: Christopher Lester (christopher@miniforge.ai)
  Copyright 2025-2026 Christopher Lester. Licensed under Apache 2.0.
-->
```

- Use copyright owner: Christopher Lester (christopher@miniforge.ai).
- Do not attribute copyright to Miniforge.ai as an entity. ✅

Apply it by hand. This rule is `strategy: manual` for the two reasons given
above; there is no machine-applicable patch to run.

